description:
crate-jmap-cid-types/src/digest.rs:9-13 (module preamble):

    > Wire format is the bare 64-character lowercase hex string — NOT
    > a wrapped JSON object. Round-trips bit-for-bit when the input
    > is already a canonical (lowercase) hex string.

…and digest.rs:86-98 (type docstring):

    > SHA-256 digest carried on the JMAP wire as a 64-character
    > lowercase hex string ...

These are testable contracts, but neither the module docstring nor the type docstring contains a doc-test (/// # Examples) demonstrating them. Round-trip / parse / serialize tests live only in #[cfg(test)] mod tests, which means:

  - cargo test --doc does not exercise them.
  - A reader scanning rustdoc on docs.rs sees no executable example of \"how do I round-trip a digest\".
  - A maintainer who breaks the round-trip contract (e.g. by accidentally normalising case at parse time) only fails the in-file unit tests, not a public API contract check.

Compare with crate-jmap-metadata-types/src/lib.rs:19-42 which carries a doc test demonstrating Annotation parse + extras preservation — visible on the type's rustdoc page and verified by `cargo test --doc`.

For a wire-format type that explicitly claims byte-for-byte round-trip fidelity, a doc test along these lines belongs in the rustdoc:

    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use jmap_cid_types::Sha256;
    /// let hex = \"e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855\";
    /// let d: Sha256 = hex.parse()?;
    /// assert_eq!(d.as_str(), hex);
    /// let json = serde_json::to_string(&d)?;
    /// assert_eq!(json, format!(\"\\\"{hex}\\\"\"));
    /// let d2: Sha256 = serde_json::from_str(&json)?;
    /// assert_eq!(d, d2);
    /// # Ok::<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>>(())
    /// ```

This is api-contract,docs because the claim is part of the contract and currently has no executable demonstration in the rustdoc layer where contract enforcement is most visible.
